# Independently authored EXTRACT(ISOYEAR) / EXTRACT(EPOCH
# FROM INTERVAL) divergence-pinning slice for the GPL
# MTR-format runner. Follow-up to PR #361 reviewer
# nice-to-haves. NOT copied or adapted from MySQL's
# mysql-test/.
#
# Existing coverage (do NOT duplicate):
#   - extract_extended_fields_basic_extra (PR #361) pins
#     ISOYEAR for `2024-05-19` and `2025-01-01` —
#     BOTH cases where ISOYEAR == YEAR (no divergence).
#     The reviewer flagged this as a coverage gap: the
#     real value of ISOYEAR is at year boundaries where
#     it diverges from YEAR. This slice pins TWO such
#     divergence cases.
#   - extract_extended_fields_basic_extra also pins
#     EPOCH for `'1 hour'` interval — doesn't exercise
#     the 1-month=30-days normalization. This slice
#     pins that quirk.
#
# This slice pins:
#   - **ISOYEAR > YEAR divergence**: `'2024-12-30'` is
#     the Monday of ISO week 1 of 2025 → ISOYEAR=2025
#     while YEAR=2024 (so ISOYEAR > YEAR).
#   - **ISOYEAR < YEAR divergence**: `'2023-01-01'` is
#     a Sunday in ISO week 52 of 2022 → ISOYEAR=2022
#     while YEAR=2023 (so ISOYEAR < YEAR).
#   - Paired ISOYEAR vs YEAR rows so a regression to a
#     silent ISOYEAR→YEAR fallback gets caught.
#   - **EPOCH(INTERVAL '1 month') = 30 * 86400 = 2592000**
#     (NOT the calendar-aware 28..31 days × 86400).
#   - **EPOCH(INTERVAL '1 year') = 12 * 30 * 86400 =
#     31104000** (NOT 365.25 × 86400 like PG would).
#
# Big-picture finding (PR #361 reviewer nice-to-haves
# made into runner-enforced pins):
#
#   **ISOYEAR vs YEAR divergence is REAL.** PR #361 only
#   exercised the equal-cases. Without these divergence
#   rows, an accidental fallback (e.g., `ISOYEAR =>
#   year_only(*days)` instead of `iso_year(y, *days)`)
#   would silently pass the existing tests. The
#   `bug_witness_*` rows here pin the actual divergence
#   so the fallback bug would surface immediately.
#
#   **EPOCH from INTERVAL uses 1-month=30-days normalization.**
#   Per impl at lib.rs:51952-51958:
#
#     total_secs = months * 30 * 86400
#                + days * 86400
#                + microseconds / 1_000_000
#
#   So `EPOCH(INTERVAL '1 month') = 2592000` (NOT the
#   calendar-aware value which would depend on the
#   reference month). Apps porting from PG (which also
#   uses 30-day normalization here) or from systems with
#   calendar-aware interval semantics need to know this.
#
#   For year intervals: `EPOCH(INTERVAL '1 year') =
#   31104000` (= 12 * 30 * 86400). PG's behavior on
#   year intervals is the same — the EPOCH function on
#   intervals is documented to use 30-day months and
#   12-month years, NOT calendar-aware.
#
#   STRIKING — these calendar quirks mean EPOCH(INTERVAL
#   '1 year') = 31104000, but `EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM
#   TIMESTAMP '2025-01-01 00:00:00') - EXTRACT(EPOCH
#   FROM TIMESTAMP '2024-01-01 00:00:00')` = 31622400
#   (366 days × 86400 — 2024 is a leap year). They
#   DIVERGE by 518400 seconds (6 days). Apps using
#   EPOCH(INTERVAL) for date math will get wrong answers
#   across leap-year boundaries.
#
# Adaptation notes:
#   # zeta-adapt: ISOYEAR vs YEAR DIVERGES at year
#     boundaries (ISO 8601 week-year). Use ISOYEAR for
#     ISO week calculations, YEAR for calendar year.
#   # zeta-adapt: EPOCH(INTERVAL '1 month') = 2592000
#     (30-day month). NOT calendar-aware.
#   # zeta-adapt: EPOCH(INTERVAL '1 year') = 31104000
#     (12 × 30 × 86400 = 360 days). Diverges from real
#     calendar year by 5-6 days.
#   # zeta-adapt: For calendar-aware interval-to-seconds,
#     subtract two timestamps and EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM ...)
#     the resulting interval is NOT the answer either —
#     use the timestamp-diff directly.

--echo === ISOYEAR > YEAR — Monday of ISO week 1 in NEXT calendar year ===

# 2024-12-30 is a Monday. ISO 8601 says: week 1 starts
# on the Monday whose week contains Jan 4. Since 2024-12-30
# is the Monday of the week containing 2025-01-01, it's
# ISO week 1 of 2025 → ISOYEAR=2025 while YEAR=2024.
SELECT EXTRACT(ISOYEAR FROM DATE '2024-12-30')                AS isoyear_2025;
SELECT EXTRACT(YEAR    FROM DATE '2024-12-30')                AS calendar_year_2024;
# Divergence witness — these MUST differ.
SELECT EXTRACT(ISOYEAR FROM DATE '2024-12-30')
    <> EXTRACT(YEAR    FROM DATE '2024-12-30')                AS bug_witness_isoyear_diverges_pos;

--echo === ISOYEAR < YEAR — Sunday in ISO week 52 of PREVIOUS year ===

# 2023-01-01 is a Sunday. It's part of the week containing
# 2022-12-26 (Monday) through 2023-01-01 — ISO week 52 of
# 2022 → ISOYEAR=2022 while YEAR=2023.
SELECT EXTRACT(ISOYEAR FROM DATE '2023-01-01')                AS isoyear_2022;
SELECT EXTRACT(YEAR    FROM DATE '2023-01-01')                AS calendar_year_2023;
SELECT EXTRACT(ISOYEAR FROM DATE '2023-01-01')
    <> EXTRACT(YEAR    FROM DATE '2023-01-01')                AS bug_witness_isoyear_diverges_neg;

--echo === ISOYEAR == YEAR control case (mid-year, no divergence) ===

# Mid-year always has ISOYEAR == YEAR. Includes this row
# so the divergence pins above are obviously distinct from
# the no-divergence case.
SELECT EXTRACT(ISOYEAR FROM DATE '2024-06-15')
     = EXTRACT(YEAR    FROM DATE '2024-06-15')                AS control_no_divergence;

--echo === EPOCH(INTERVAL '1 month') = 30 * 86400 = 2592000 ===

# 1-month-as-30-days normalization (lib.rs:51952-51958).
SELECT EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM INTERVAL '1 month')                 AS one_month_2592000;
# 2 months = 60 * 86400.
SELECT EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM INTERVAL '2 months')                AS two_months_5184000;
# 12 months EXPLICITLY = 360 * 86400 (12 * 30 day-equivalent).
SELECT EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM INTERVAL '12 months')               AS twelve_months_31104000;

--echo === EPOCH(INTERVAL '1 year') = 12 * 30 * 86400 = 31104000 ===

# Year intervals normalize to 12 months = 360 days.
SELECT EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM INTERVAL '1 year')                  AS one_year_31104000;
# Equivalence: '1 year' ≡ '12 months' for EPOCH purposes.
SELECT EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM INTERVAL '1 year')
     = EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM INTERVAL '12 months')               AS year_eq_12_months_epoch;

--echo === STRIKING: leap-year divergence from real-calendar second-count ===

# Real calendar: 2024-01-01 → 2025-01-01 is 366 days (2024
# is a leap year) = 31622400 seconds.
SELECT EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM TIMESTAMP '2025-01-01 00:00:00')
     - EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM TIMESTAMP '2024-01-01 00:00:00')    AS leap_year_real_31622400;
# EPOCH(INTERVAL '1 year') normalized = 31104000.
SELECT EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM INTERVAL '1 year')                  AS interval_year_normalized_31104000;
# Divergence = 518400 seconds = 6 days.
SELECT (EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM TIMESTAMP '2025-01-01 00:00:00')
      - EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM TIMESTAMP '2024-01-01 00:00:00'))
      - EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM INTERVAL '1 year')                 AS divergence_518400_seconds;

--echo === EPOCH on mixed-component interval — additive composition ===

# (months=1) + (days=15) + (hours=2)
#  = 30*86400 + 15*86400 + 2*3600
#  = 2592000 + 1296000 + 7200
#  = 3895200
SELECT EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM INTERVAL '1 month 15 days 2 hours') AS composed_3895200;

--echo === EPOCH on negative interval ===

# Negative interval → negative seconds (sign propagates).
SELECT EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM INTERVAL '-1 hour')                 AS neg_3600;
SELECT EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM INTERVAL '-1 month')                AS neg_2592000;
